(:The problem

After upgrading the control plane to 1.32.13, the kube-proxy pods on every node were still running the old version (1.31.9).

(:Why it happened

kubeadm did its part. It updated the kube-proxy definition (the DaemonSet) to say “use version 1.32.13”. But Kubernetes then has to replace the old pods with new ones, and it does that carefully, one pod at a time.

Here’s what blocked it:
Your cluster has 10 nodes, and 5 are NotReady (down).
Kubernetes already counts those 5 kube-proxy pods as “unavailable”.
The rollout rule says only 1 pod may be unavailable (maxUnavailable: 1).
5 is already more than 1, so Kubernetes refuses to replace any pod.

Your output showed this exactly: UP-TO-DATE 0, READY 5, and rollout status timing out.

An analogy: a shop says “only 1 cashier may be on break at a time”. But 5 are already absent, so nobody else is ever allowed to take a break, and the schedule is stuck.

(:How we solve it

Raise the limit so Kubernetes can continue updating the healthy nodes:
kubectl -n kube-system patch ds kube-proxy -p '{"spec":{"updateStrategy":{"type":"RollingUpdate","rollingUpdate":{"maxUnavailable":6}}}}'

Using the analogy: 5 are already absent, so we allow 6 absent. That lets 1 more healthy cashier take a break and be replaced, one at a time.

Important: it isn’t fixed yet

So far we’ve only found the cause and the fix. You haven’t run the patch, so kube-proxy is still on 1.31.9. After you run it, verify:

kubectl -n kube-system get pods -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName,IMAGE:.spec.containers[*].image | grep kube-proxy

Success looks like: the 5 healthy nodes show v1.32.13. The 5 NotReady nodes will still show the old version, which is normal because they’re down.

(:The root cause
The real underlying problem is the 5 NotReady nodes. The patch is a workaround. As long as those nodes are down, they’ll cause the same trouble again with Calico and with the next upgrade. The permanent fix is to repair them or remove them from the cluster.

